          "answer": "**Urgency: routine.** The red-flag check found no emergency pattern in the description you gave. That is not a clearance. The check only covers a fixed list of presentations, so if symptoms become severe, worsen, or feel new and unexplained, seek medical advice promptly. If you ever have chest pain, fainting, severe breathlessness, or thoughts of harming yourself, get urgent help right away.\n\n**Body systems:** The symptom pattern did not match a specific body system, so no system grouping was produced. Persistent worry that affects work is a common reason to speak with a clinician. The general reference on generalized anxiety disorder describes persistent, hard-to-control worry about many everyday matters, usually lasting six months or more and causing impairment. It is often accompanied by restlessness, fatigue, poor concentration, irritability, muscle tension, and disturbed sleep. Only a clinician can judge whether that description fits your situation.\n\n**Things a clinician may want to check:** The retrieved material on insomnia notes that anxiety, depression, caffeine, alcohol, nicotine, stimulants, some prescribed medications, and sleep disorders can all overlap with sleep problems, so they are usually screened for. The hypertension reference lists chronic stress among contributors to high blood pressure, but it does not say stress causes anxiety. I could not retrieve further sources on medical causes of anxiety, because my search budget for this answer ran out. Please ask a clinician about the full range of possible causes.\n\nA reasonable next step is to book a appointment with a GP or primary care clinician and describe how long this has gone on and how it affects your work. Keeping a short note of when the anxiety happens and what you notice physically can help that conversation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
