          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What happens if the score is high?",
          "answer": "I need to know which score you mean and what the value was. A high result means different things on different tests. Tell me the name of the test (for example, a screening questionnaire, a risk score, or a lab result) and the number, and I can explain what that specific result means.\n\nIn general, a high result on a screening questionnaire prompts further assessment. It is not a diagnosis. The anxiety screening guidance says a score \"is not a diagnosis\" and that it prompts a diagnostic assessment. That assessment usually includes ruling out physical causes such as thyroid disease and heart rhythm problems, reviewing caffeine, substances, and medicines, and checking for depression, which often occurs alongside anxiety. The same guidance says a score of 10 is the point at which further assessment usually follows. It names cognitive behavioral therapy and certain prescribed medicines as first-line options, chosen together with the person. (Source: guideline-anxiety-screening-and-first-line-care.)\n\nOther scores work differently. In COPD, a symptom questionnaire is one of two factors used to place a person in a treatment group. A high symptom score alone does not set the treatment. (Source: guideline-copd-diagnosis-and-stepwise-management.) For blood pressure, the threshold that counts as high depends on which guidance is used. US and European guidance use different cut-offs. (Source: guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets.)\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
